  "status": "draft",
  "title": "Dot diagrams, histograms and stem-and-leaf diagrams",
  "summary": "Read distributions accurately from dot diagrams, histograms and stem-and-leaf diagrams, including unequal class widths.",
  "outcomes": [
    "1.5"
  ],
  "prerequisite_lessons": [],
  "estimated_minutes": 55,
  "objectives": [
    "Read frequencies, clusters, gaps and spread from dot diagrams.",
    "Decode ordered values and keys in stem-and-leaf diagrams.",
    "Interpret histogram area and frequency density when class widths differ."
  ],
  "mastery_policy_key": "s1-default-v1",
  "sections": [
    {
      "stable_key": "s1-05-explanation-1",
      "position": 1,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Read a dot diagram",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A dot diagram (also called a dot plot) shows each observation as one dot above a number line. Read the horizontal scale first, then count stacked dots to obtain a frequency. The layout exposes repeated values, gaps, clusters and possible outliers while preserving every observation. Range is the greatest value minus the least value."
        },
        {
          "type": "asset_ref",
          "asset_key": "s1-lesson-05-diagram"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "s1-05-example-dot",
      "position": 2,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: read a dot diagram",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The dot diagram shows the number of hours of sport that 10 students played in one week. Find the mode and the range, and describe any gap."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Count the dots above each value. The tallest stack is three dots above 4, so the mode is 4 hours."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The least value is 2 and the greatest is 9."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "\\text{range}=9-2=7\\text{ hours}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No student played 7 or 8 hours, so the value 9 stands apart from the main cluster from 2 to 6."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "There are 1 + 2 + 3 + 2 + 1 + 1 = 10 dots, one for each student."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "s1-05-explanation-2",
      "position": 3,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Decode stem-and-leaf values",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A stem-and-leaf diagram orders exact observations while showing distribution shape. The key is essential: 4|7 may mean 47, 4.7 or another scale. For back-to-back diagrams, both leaf sets share the same stem but extend in opposite directions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "s1-05-explanation-3",
      "position": 4,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Use area in a histogram",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Histogram bars touch because the variable is continuous. With equal class widths, frequency comparisons can use bar heights. With unequal widths, bar area represents frequency and height represents frequency density: frequency density = frequency divided by class width."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "s1-05-example-1",
      "position": 5,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: interpret unequal widths",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A class of width 5 has frequency density 4 and a class of width 10 has frequency density 2. Find both frequencies."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Use frequency = class width × frequency density."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "5\\times4=20,\\qquad 10\\times2=20"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "Both bars have area 20 although the first is twice as tall, which is why area, not height, shows frequency here."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "s1-05-example-2",
      "position": 6,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: read a stem-and-leaf diagram",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The row 3 | 1 4 4 8 has key 3 | 1 = 31. Find its median and range."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The ordered values are 31, 34, 34 and 38."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "There are four values, so the median is the mean of the middle two."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "\\frac{34+34}{2}=34"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "\\text{range}=38-31=7"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "Two values lie below 34 and two at or above it, and 31 + 7 = 38."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "s1-05-recall",
      "position": 7,
      "type": "active_recall",
      "title": "Check your understanding",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A class of width 6 has frequency density 3. Find its frequency."
        }
      ],
      "response": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "comparison_mode": "exact_numeric",
        "canonical_answer": "18",
        "canonical_latex": "18",
        "accepted_answers": [],
        "absolute_tolerance": null,
        "rounding_precision": null,
        "unit": null
      },
      "feedback": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Frequency equals class width multiplied by density: 6×3=18."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "s1-05-summary",
      "position": 8,
      "type": "summary",
      "title": "Lesson checklist",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Dot diagram: count the dots at each value to read frequencies, the mode, the range and gaps. Stem-and-leaf diagram: use the key to decode exact values, already in order. Histogram: with unequal class widths, bar area shows frequency and height shows frequency density."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Read the scale or key first, then calculate, and say what the result means for the data."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
